{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Judah said to him, saying.</b> The verse's word <b>le'mor</b> means — that Judah said to him a presentation of words, to say to him the reply, and the presentation is ״הָעֵד הֵעִיד״ etc., and the saying is ״אִם יֶשְׁךָ״ etc. And the verse intends by this to remove a reply from any other implication heard from the aforementioned presentation, namely that since ״he testified״ etc., they are perforce required to take Benjamin and go, whether willingly or unwillingly. Therefore he said ״le'mor״ — meaning, this declaration, its decree is what he says afterward." "<b>Or</b> it may intend in this manner: <b>le'mor, testifying</b> etc., meaning he said to us this and testified against us with witnesses in his warning. It further intends to say: Answer me regarding this claim — if you have a reply to give to the lord of the land. It further intends that there is yet another argument to make besides this, namely the danger to Shimon, who is given over in prison — a compelling proof that they are not spies." "<b>You shall not see my face, etc.</b> By this he intends to remove the claim that Jacob might make — that he would produce proofs and evidence sufficient before kings and counselors of the land to establish that they are not suspected spies, and thereby the claim of the man who suspects them would be settled. Therefore he said <b>he testified against us, saying: You shall not see</b>, meaning that he is not willing to see our faces at all without the coming of Benjamin, and from now on they cannot stand before him in any manner, so how would they go to purchase grain from him. And perhaps it is for this that Judah repeated this matter twice, for this condition of Benjamin is binding even if the suspicion of spying were removed."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:18.805083182Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}